  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2026 Community Feedback Data Lab training</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">20&#x2013;25 participants: CEA surge roster members, National Society staff already implementing feedback systems under DREF operations, and relevant IFRC regional/country CEA technical staff. The note is explicit that this isn&apos;t introductory; it&apos;s aimed at people who&apos;ve already worked with feedback data and want to sharpen analytical and communication skills, with the goal of building a deployable cohort with demonstrated competency.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A 4-day hybrid workshop called the Community Feedback Data Lab, teaching CEA (Community Engagement and Accountability) practitioners how to analyze community feedback data and turn it into operational decisions; the part of feedback systems that consistently fails in practice.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Because collecting feedback during emergencies has become standard practice, but acting on it hasn&apos;t. The note identifies this gap explicitly: teams lack the skills, tools, and confidence to track and analyze feedback and convert it into decisions. Rather than running a generic training, the design grounds everything in one real, documented case (the Al Haouz earthquake response) so participants work with actual operational messiness rather than a hypothetical scenario.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Morocco, hosted by the Moroccan Red Crescent and IFRC.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through a progressive structure rather than a standard classroom format: foundational concepts first, then hands-on analysis of the real Morocco dataset, then translating findings into recommendations pitched to sectors and leadership, and finally a field day where participants meet implementing staff and reflect on what they&apos;d do differently. The mechanism is co-design and co-delivery between IFRC&apos;s CEA unit, Swedish Red Cross, and Netherlands Red Cross, with Moroccan Red Crescent staff as learning partners rather than passive hosts.</narrative>
  </description>
